  "prediction": "**Seven Men Charged in 2014 Hazing Death, Fraternity Sues for Name Misuse**\n\nIn a shocking turn of events, seven men have been charged in connection with the 2014 hazing death of University at Albany student Trevor Duffy. The men allegedly forced Duffy to drink a 60-ounce bottle of vodka, leading to his tragic death. The fraternity the men claim to be part of, Zeta Beta Tau (ZBT), has released a statement denying any affiliation with the group.\n\nAccording to ZBT, the men were not part of the Epsilon Gamma Chapter, which is recognized by the university and the national fraternity. Instead, they were a rogue group that used the ZBT name without authorization. ZBT has retained legal counsel and plans to sue the men for falsely representing themselves and damaging the fraternity's reputation.\n\nThe lawsuit claims that the men's unauthorized use of the ZBT name, symbols, and materials is a violation of United States Copyright and Trademark law. ZBT has stated that it will take legal action against anyone who uses its intellectual property without permission.\n\nThe seven men charged in connection with Duffy's death include Joseph Angilletta, who is represented by Attorney Paul DerOhannesian. DerOhannesian has stated that his client is not ready to comment on the new allegations, but has expressed surprise at ZBT's decision to sue.\n\nZBT, founded in 1898 in New York City, is a Jewish fraternity with chapters on over 90 campuses across North America and more than 130,000 undergraduate and alumni brothers worldwide. The fraternity has a strong reputation and is known for its commitment to academic excellence and community service.\n\nThe charges against the seven men are a significant development in the case, and the lawsuit filed by ZBT is a clear indication of the fraternity's determination to protect its name and reputation. The case is ongoing, and it remains to be seen how it will unfold.",
